const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is a dog body condition score chart?',
    a: 'A dog body condition score (BCS) chart rates fat cover on a 1–9 scale (some clinics use 1–5). Score 4–5 is typically ideal: ribs easy to feel under a thin fat layer, a waist when viewed from above, and an abdominal tuck from the side. Lower scores mean too thin; higher scores mean overweight or obese. BCS is a screening tool, not a lab diagnosis.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is my dog overweight if I cannot feel the ribs?',
    a: 'Difficulty feeling ribs under a thick fat pad often lands around BCS 7–9 and suggests excess weight, but thick coats and breed shape can confuse beginners. Ask your veterinarian to demonstrate scoring on your dog. Confirm with weight trends and, when needed, diagnostic workups for disease—not internet photos alone.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is BCS different from dog BMI?',
    a: 'Human-style BMI uses weight and height and is only a rough index in dogs. BCS uses hands-on and visual fat-cover cues that map better to clinical teaching charts such as WSAVA’s. Our dog BMI calculator offers an educational index plus a suggested BCS band; the chart on this page explains what each score looks and feels like in words.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How fast should an overweight dog lose weight?',
    a: 'Safe rates are individualized. Many veterinary weight programs aim for gradual loss with measured calories, not crash starvation. Too-fast loss can be unhealthy. Your veterinarian should set the target weight, diet, and recheck schedule—especially for dogs with medical conditions.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can a dog be skinny but still unhealthy?',
    a: 'Yes. Low BCS can reflect underfeeding, parasites, dental pain, diabetes, cancer, or other disease. Sudden weight loss needs a veterinary visit even if you hoped for a “leaner look.” Puppies that stay ribby with pot bellies need parasite and diet checks.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do greyhounds and bulldogs score the same way?',
    a: 'The 1–9 ideas (ribs, waist, tummy tuck) still apply, but breed silhouette differs. Sight hounds naturally look leaner; some brachycephalic or deep-chested breeds hide waists. Ask a clinician familiar with your breed to calibrate your eye.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I use treats if my dog is overweight?',
    a: 'Training treats can stay in the plan if they count inside the daily calorie budget—often keeping extras near 10% of calories. Swap to tiny pieces of regular kibble when possible. Recalculate meals with the dog food calculator after your veterinarian sets a target.',
